{
  "id": "merge-intervals",
  "title": "Merge Intervals",
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "categories": ["intervals", "sorting", "array"],
  "descriptionMd": "You are given a list of closed intervals, where `intervals[i] = [start, end]`. The list is **not necessarily sorted**.\n\nCombine every group of overlapping intervals into a single interval and return the resulting non-overlapping intervals **sorted by start**. Intervals that merely touch, such as `[1, 4]` and `[4, 5]`, count as overlapping.\n\n**Example 1**\n\n```\nintervals = [[1, 3], [2, 6], [8, 10], [15, 18]]\n→ [[1, 6], [8, 10], [15, 18]]\n```\n`[1, 3]` and `[2, 6]` overlap, so they become `[1, 6]`.\n\n**Example 2**\n\n```\nintervals = [[1, 4], [4, 5]]\n→ [[1, 5]]\n```\nThe intervals share the point 4, so they merge.",
  "constraints": ["1 ≤ intervals.length ≤ 10⁴", "intervals[i].length == 2", "0 ≤ start ≤ end ≤ 10⁴"],
  "hints": ["Overlapping intervals are hard to spot in a jumbled list. What ordering would put them next to each other?", "Once ordered, walk through the list and decide for each interval whether it extends the last merged one or starts a new one."],
